<!--hvy: {"id":"overview","expanded":true}-->
#! Overview

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # HVY File And Attachment Save/Load Lifecycle

  A saved `.hvy` file is one byte stream. Its readable HVY text comes first, followed by an optional attachment envelope
  and opaque binary payloads. Attachments are not separate sidecar files unless a host deliberately publishes them that
  way.

 <!--hvy:plugin {"plugin":"hvy.diagram","pluginConfig":{"syntax":"mermaid"}}-->
  flowchart LR
    text[HVY text body] --> directives[Tail directives]
    directives --> sentinel[--HVY-TAIL-- sentinel]
    sentinel --> payloads[Concatenated attachment bytes]

<!--hvy: {"id":"file-layout","expanded":true}-->
#! Saved File Layout

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Saved File Layout

  Each `hvy:tail` directive records a stable attachment `id`, metadata such as `mediaType`, and the byte `length`. The
  payloads follow the sentinel in directive order. The length values define the byte slices, so payload bytes never need
  to be interpreted as text.

  Common IDs include `image:<filename>`, `db`, `encrypted:<keyId>`, and derived embedding-index IDs. IDs are unique within
  a document. Writing an existing ID replaces that attachment while preserving a single canonical entry.

 <!--hvy:plugin {"plugin":"hvy.diagram","pluginConfig":{"syntax":"mermaid"}}-->
  flowchart TD
    body[Serialized header, sections, and components]
    d1[hvy:tail id image:cover.png length N]
    d2[hvy:tail id db length M]
    marker[--HVY-TAIL--]
    imageBytes[N image bytes]
    dbBytes[M database bytes]
    body --> d1 --> d2 --> marker --> imageBytes --> dbBytes

<!--hvy: {"id":"loading","expanded":true}-->
#! Loading A HVY File

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Loading A HVY File

  File-backed HVY documents must be loaded as bytes with `deserializeDocumentBytes`. The loader locates the tail
  boundary, parses only the preceding text as HVY, and indexes each payload as a lazy byte slice in the document's
  `AttachmentStore`. Listing descriptors therefore does not materialize every attachment. Calling `get`, `recall`, or a
  component-specific attachment helper materializes only the requested bytes.

  Loading a real `.hvy` file as raw text is incorrect: decoding binary tail bytes can corrupt them or expose them in the
  visible document. Text deserialization is suitable only when the caller actually has a text-only representation.

 <!--hvy:plugin {"plugin":"hvy.diagram","pluginConfig":{"syntax":"mermaid"}}-->
  flowchart TD
    bytes[Complete HVY byte stream] --> encrypted{Encrypted envelope?}
    encrypted -->|Yes| decrypt[Decrypt to standard HVY bytes]
    encrypted -->|No| split[Find tail directives and sentinel]
    decrypt --> split
    split --> text[Decode and parse text region]
    split --> slices[Index lazy attachment byte slices]
    text --> document[VisualDocument]
    slices --> store[AttachmentStore]
    store --> document
    document --> render[Components request attachments by stable ID]

<!--hvy: {"id":"in-memory-store","expanded":true}-->
#! In-Memory Attachment Store

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # In-Memory Attachment Store

  Every loaded document is attached to one `AttachmentStore`. The document's `attachments` property is a compatibility
  view backed by that store; the store is the authoritative in-memory index. Descriptors contain `id`, metadata, and
  length. Entries may hold materialized bytes or a reference to a slice of the original file bytes.

  Components and plugins should use stable IDs rather than retaining independent byte copies. Image and carousel
  components both resolve `image:<filename>`. Plugin-owned data uses plugin-defined stable IDs and metadata.

<!--hvy: {"id":"saving","expanded":true}-->
#! Saving A HVY File

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Saving A HVY File

  `serializeDocumentBytes` serializes the document text, appends tail directives built from the current attachment
  descriptors, writes the sentinel, and then appends every attachment payload in the same order. Unknown attachment
  types are preserved because serialization copies their bytes without interpreting them.

  A save request does not itself choose storage. The reference app writes to the current writable file when available
  and otherwise asks before downloading. Embedded hosts receive the serialized byte stream and decide whether to write a
  file, database record, object-store object, or another destination.

 <!--hvy:plugin {"plugin":"hvy.diagram","pluginConfig":{"syntax":"mermaid"}}-->
  flowchart TD
    edit[Document, component, or plugin edit] --> store[Update AttachmentStore by ID]
    store --> request[Explicit save or serialization request]
    request --> prepare[Prepare encrypted and derived attachments]
    prepare --> serialize[Serialize HVY text and attachment descriptors]
    serialize --> recall[Recall attachment bytes in descriptor order]
    recall --> output[Complete HVY byte stream]
    output --> destination{Save destination}
    destination -->|Reference app| file[Writable source file or download]
    destination -->|Embedded host| host[Host-managed persistence]

<!--hvy: {"id":"host-managed-attachments","expanded":true}-->
#! Host-Managed Attachments

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Host-Managed Attachments

  Embedded hosts may provide an attachment adapter with `list`, `recall`, `store`, and `remove`. An optional `resolveUrl`
  lets image renderers use a host URL without first downloading bytes into the document. This is useful for large or
  remotely stored assets.

  When attachment recall or final serialization is asynchronous, hosts use `serializeDocumentBytesAsync` and may supply
  a serializer adapter. The serializer receives the text body, ordered descriptors, and a recall function. The required
  result is still a complete valid HVY byte stream unless the host is intentionally producing the documented hosted
  extraction layout.

 <!--hvy:plugin {"plugin":"hvy.diagram","pluginConfig":{"syntax":"mermaid"}}-->
  sequenceDiagram
    participant HVY as Mounted HVY client
    participant Adapter as Attachment adapter
    participant Host as Host serializer/storage
    HVY->>Adapter: list descriptors
    HVY->>Host: text body + ordered tail descriptors
    Host->>HVY: recallAttachment(id)
    HVY->>Adapter: recall(id)
    Adapter-->>HVY: attachment bytes
    HVY-->>Host: attachment bytes
    Host-->>HVY: complete saved HVY bytes

<!--hvy: {"id":"invariants","expanded":true}-->
#! Required Invariants

 <!--hvy:text {}-->
  # Required Invariants

  - Load file-backed HVY documents from bytes, not raw text.
  - Keep one attachment store attached to the active document.
  - Address attachments by stable, document-unique IDs.
  - Preserve unknown descriptors, metadata, order, and bytes on save.
  - Keep descriptor lengths synchronized with the bytes that will be written.
  - Serialize text, directives, sentinel, and payload bytes as one coherent stream.
  - Use asynchronous serialization whenever attachment recall or host serialization is asynchronous.
  - Do not treat rendering failure as permission to delete or omit an attachment.
